  <main class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-6 py-16"><a href="/blog" class="text-orange-400 text-sm">← Back to the blog</a><h1 class="text-4xl font-bold text-white mt-6 mb-4">1984.is VPS Review 2026: Iceland Privacy Hosting</h1><p class="text-gray-500 mb-10">Updated August 3, 2026 · VPS Review</p>
  <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-6">1984.is occupies a different corner of the VPS market: it sells an Iceland-based hosting story built around privacy, renewable electricity, and independent operation rather than a race to the lowest introductory price. The reviewed entry plan is listed at $9.66 per month, but the important qualifier is prepaid billing for one or three years.</p>
  <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mt-10 mb-4">The plan in plain English</h2><p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">The Grunnpakki entry tier includes 1 vCPU, 1GB RAM, 25GB NVMe SSD storage, and 1TB of monthly transfer. It is a sensible footprint for a personal VPN, monitoring stack, small web service, or lightweight automation worker. NVMe storage should keep package installs and ordinary database work responsive, but this is not a large-memory or high-throughput machine.</p><table class="w-full text-sm text-left mb-8"><thead><tr class="border-b border-gray-700"><th class="py-3">Item</th><th>Listed detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr class="border-b border-gray-800"><td class="py-3">Location</td><td>Reykjavik, Iceland</td></tr><tr class="border-b border-gray-800"><td class="py-3">Entry price</td><td>$9.66/mo prepaid</td></tr><tr class="border-b border-gray-800"><td class="py-3">Storage</td><td>25GB NVMe SSD</td></tr><tr class="border-b border-gray-800"><td class="py-3">Transfer</td><td>1TB/mo metered</td></tr></tbody></table>
  <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mt-10 mb-4">Why privacy is the main differentiator</h2><p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">The provider states a no-logging policy for its VPS product and operates from Iceland. That does not make a server magically anonymous: the customer still controls the operating system, SSH keys, applications, and logs. It does, however, give privacy-conscious buyers a clearer jurisdiction and policy story than a generic low-cost cloud instance.</p><p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">The company also highlights ISO 27001 security management, green Icelandic power, and Bitcoin and Monero payments for eligible non-local customers. Treat these as useful signals, not substitutes for your own threat model. If you need a formal uptime remedy, ask for the current VPS SLA before committing.</p>
  <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mt-10 mb-4">The trade-offs</h2><p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">A Reykjavik-only entry location is the biggest practical constraint. European users may find the latency acceptable, while users serving North American or Asian audiences should measure round-trip times before migrating a production workload. The 1TB transfer allowance also makes the plan a poor fit for media delivery or public mirrors.</p><p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">Prepaid billing reduces month-to-month flexibility. The effective monthly price is straightforward, but the upfront commitment means you should test a small workload first and confirm refund terms. Plan visibility is also less polished than the self-service catalogs offered by large cloud platforms.</p>
  <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mt-10 mb-4">Verdict</h2><p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-6">Choose 1984.is when Icelandic jurisdiction, a stated no-logging posture, renewable energy, and an independent operator matter more than the cheapest possible VPS. Skip it for globally distributed applications, heavy transfer, or month-to-month experimentation. For buyers who decide the location is a fit, the 4.3/5 rating reflects a distinctive privacy proposition with real billing and regional limitations—not a universal budget winner.</p><p class="text-gray-500 text-sm">Disclosure: VPSTier may earn a commission from clearly labeled alternative links. That does not change the pricing or limitations described above.</p></main>
